From: NanoKVM build <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: cv1800b_sdhci: program the PHY at init, honour max-frequency

Two independent bugs that together made U-Boot's SD reads on this SoC
unreliable while Linux's were fine on the same card.

1. The PHY was never programmed.

   Linux configures the CV18xx PHY from cv18xx_sdhci_reset(), which the
   SDHCI core calls at init and on every mode change: MSHC_CTRL gets
   LATANCY_1T, PHY_CONFIG gets TX_BPS, and PHY_TX_RX_DLY gets both source
   clocks inverted with zero delay. U-Boot's driver only ever wrote
   PHY_TX_RX_DLY, and only from platform_execute_tuning() -- which never
   runs on a 3.3V-only board, because no-1-8-v masks out every UHS mode
   that would trigger tuning. So on the LicheeRV Nano the delay lines kept
   whatever the FSBL left, and reads at SD high-speed failed intermittently
   ("Invalid FAT entry", "Error reading cluster", "No partition table") on
   media that reads back byte-correct.

   set_control_reg is the hook the core calls on each set_ios before it
   programs the clock, which is the closest analogue U-Boot has to the
   kernel's ->reset. Note it deliberately does not chain to the generic
   sdhci_set_control_reg(): this driver has never installed that op, so the
   voltage-switch and UHS-timing handling has never run here, and on a
   no-1-8-v board sdhci_set_voltage() is the very CMD11 path that wedges
   the card.

2. max-frequency was silently discarded.

   mmc_of_parse() puts the DT property into cfg->f_max, and then
   sdhci_setup_cfg() overwrites cfg->f_max with host->max_clk whenever its
   own f_max argument is zero. This driver passed zero, so max-frequency
   never had any effect and the bus ran at whatever the negotiated mode
   allowed. Pass the parsed value through.

   This matters beyond tidiness: it is the safety valve. If the PHY setup
   above ever proves insufficient on some card, capping sdhci0 in the board
   DTS now actually caps it.

While here, derive the CV18xx register offsets from the DWC vendor-area-1
pointer at 0xe8 instead of hardcoding 0x240, matching the Linux driver. The
pointer reads 0x200 on this SoC, so the resulting address is unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Pending [should go to u-boot; not yet submitted]
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/cv1800b_sdhci.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/cv1800b_sdhci.c
@@ -6,13 +6,82 @@
 #include <dm.h>
 #include <mmc.h>
 #include <sdhci.h>
+#include <linux/bitops.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 
-#define SDHCI_PHY_TX_RX_DLY  0x240
 #define MMC_MAX_CLOCK        375000000
 #define TUNE_MAX_PHCODE      128
 
-#define PHY_TX_SRC_INVERT  BIT(8)
+/*
+ * The Sophgo PHY registers live inside the DWC IP's vendor area 1, whose
+ * offset the controller reports at 0xe8. It reads 0x200 on CV18xx/SG2002, so
+ * PHY_TX_RX_DLY lands at the 0x240 this driver used to hardcode. Reading the
+ * pointer keeps the names and the arithmetic identical to the Linux driver
+ * (drivers/mmc/host/sdhci-of-dwcmshc.c), which is the reference for what these
+ * bits have to be set to.
+ */
+#define DWCMSHC_P_VENDOR_AREA1  0xe8
+#define DWCMSHC_AREA1_MASK      GENMASK(11, 0)
+
+#define CV18XX_MSHC_CTRL        0x00
+#define  CV18XX_LATANCY_1T      BIT(1)
+#define CV18XX_PHY_TX_RX_DLY    0x40
+#define  PHY_TX_SRC_INVERT      BIT(8)
+#define  PHY_RX_SRC_INVERT      BIT(24)
+#define CV18XX_PHY_CONFIG       0x4c
+#define  CV18XX_PHY_TX_BPS      BIT(0)
+
+static int cv1800b_vendor_area1(struct sdhci_host *host)
+{
+	return sdhci_readl(host, DWCMSHC_P_VENDOR_AREA1) & DWCMSHC_AREA1_MASK;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Program the PHY the way the Linux driver's cv18xx_sdhci_reset() does.
+ *
+ * Without this the PHY keeps whatever the FSBL left behind: TX/RX clocks
+ * un-inverted, the 1T latency bit clear and the TX bypass off. Reads then work
+ * at default speed and are marginal at SD high-speed -- "Invalid FAT entry",
+ * "Error reading cluster", "No partition table" on media that is byte-correct.
+ * The driver only ever touched these registers from platform_execute_tuning(),
+ * which never runs on a 3.3V-only board because no-1-8-v masks out every UHS
+ * mode. So on this board they were never programmed at all.
+ *
+ * set_control_reg is the one hook the SDHCI core calls on each set_ios before
+ * it programs the clock, which makes it the closest thing U-Boot has to the
+ * kernel's ->reset callback. It deliberately does NOT chain to
+ * sdhci_set_control_reg(): this driver has never had a set_control_reg op, so
+ * the generic voltage-switch and UHS-timing handling has never run here, and
+ * on a no-1-8-v board sdhci_set_voltage() is exactly the CMD11 path that
+ * wedges the card (see the no-1-8-v quirk below).
+ */
+static void cv1800b_sdhci_phy_init(struct sdhci_host *host)
+{
+	int area = cv1800b_vendor_area1(host);
+	u16 clk;
+	u32 val;
+
+	/* Gate the card clock across the PHY writes, as cv18xx_sdhci_set_tap()
+	 * does; the delay lines must not move under a running clock.
+	 */
+	clk = sdhci_readw(host, SDHCI_CLOCK_CONTROL);
+	sdhci_writew(host, clk & ~SDHCI_CLOCK_CARD_EN, SDHCI_CLOCK_CONTROL);
+
+	val = sdhci_readl(host, area + CV18XX_MSHC_CTRL);
+	val |= CV18XX_LATANCY_1T;
+	sdhci_writel(host, val, area + CV18XX_MSHC_CTRL);
+
+	val = sdhci_readl(host, area + CV18XX_PHY_CONFIG);
+	val |= CV18XX_PHY_TX_BPS;
+	sdhci_writel(host, val, area + CV18XX_PHY_CONFIG);
+
+	/* TX delay 0 / RX delay 0, both source clocks inverted. */
+	sdhci_writel(host, PHY_TX_SRC_INVERT | PHY_RX_SRC_INVERT,
+		     area + CV18XX_PHY_TX_RX_DLY);
+
+	sdhci_writew(host, clk, SDHCI_CLOCK_CONTROL);
+	udelay(1000);
+}
 
 struct cv1800b_sdhci_plat {
 	struct mmc_config cfg;
@@ -22,7 +91,8 @@
 #if CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(MMC_SUPPORTS_TUNING)
 static void cv1800b_set_tap_delay(struct sdhci_host *host, u16 tap)
 {
-	sdhci_writel(host, PHY_TX_SRC_INVERT | tap << 16, SDHCI_PHY_TX_RX_DLY);
+	sdhci_writel(host, PHY_TX_SRC_INVERT | tap << 16,
+		     cv1800b_vendor_area1(host) + CV18XX_PHY_TX_RX_DLY);
 }
 
 static void cv1800b_sdhci_reset(struct sdhci_host *host, u8 mask)
@@ -65,6 +135,7 @@
 #endif
 
 const struct sdhci_ops cv1800b_sdhci_sd_ops = {
+	.set_control_reg = cv1800b_sdhci_phy_init,
 #if CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(MMC_SUPPORTS_TUNING)
 	.platform_execute_tuning = cv1800b_execute_tuning,
 #endif
@@ -109,7 +180,14 @@
 	if (dev_read_bool(dev, "no-1-8-v"))
 		host->quirks |= SDHCI_QUIRK_NO_1_8_V;
 
-	ret = sdhci_setup_cfg(&plat->cfg, host, 0, 200000);
+	/*
+	 * Pass the parsed f_max back in rather than 0. mmc_of_parse() has
+	 * already put the DT "max-frequency" into cfg->f_max, but
+	 * sdhci_setup_cfg() overwrites cfg->f_max with host->max_clk whenever
+	 * its f_max argument is zero -- so with 0 the DT property was silently
+	 * discarded and the bus ran at whatever the negotiated mode allowed.
+	 */
+	ret = sdhci_setup_cfg(&plat->cfg, host, plat->cfg.f_max, 200000);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
